<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burma Socialist Programme Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1962">1962</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as BSPP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> Socialist Programme Party was the ruling party of Burma under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a>. Founded by the Revolutionary Council in 1962, it became the sole legal party in 1964 and under the 1974 constitution ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-republic-of-the-union-of-burma">Socialist Republic of the Union of Burma</a>, following the ideology of the 'Burmese Way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a>'. It was swept away by the uprising and military takeover of 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burma-socialist-programme-party.jpg" alt="Burma Socialist Programme Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Burma_Socialist_Programme_Party_(1962%E2%80%931988).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
